# Owner correction — Fixer refusal has one narrow purpose

The broad notion of a "safe refusal" is rejected.

Fixer performs its assigned professional work until it completes. Difficulty, size, elapsed or expected effort, incomplete work, uncertainty, root-cause investigation, failing tests, context pressure, or a wish to avoid a false completion claim never authorize refusal. If execution or infrastructure collapses, the invocation exits nonzero; it does not submit a professional refusal. A caller may externally stop its budget, which is cancellation/failure, not refusal.

Fixer refusal has one narrow purpose: reject an assignment that is not presently lawfully executable. Its only typed causes are:

1. `authority_violation`: the requested work contradicts or exceeds governing authority/constitution.
2. `prerequisite_unmet`: a specifically identified required predecessor decision/artifact/state has not completed.

These are two evidence forms of one status: assignment inadmissible. There is no `unsafe_repository_state`, `insufficient_evidence`, `workload`, `timeout`, `capacity`, `false_or_stale_premise`, or generic safety category. Tool and infrastructure failures cannot become refusal.

Use subtraction rather than adding broad anti-refusal prose: define the refusal domain as exactly those two causes and expose no other cause.

Fixer apply has three professional settlements:

- `completed`: every packet finding is completed.
- `refused`: no packet finding is completed; every finding is refused for exactly `authority_violation` or `prerequisite_unmet`.
- `partially_completed`: at least one finding is completed and at least one finding is refused for exactly one of those causes.

A partial settlement is mixed lawful disposition, not unfinished workload. Per-finding typed results distinguish completed findings from refused findings. If any executable finding merely remains unfinished, there is no professional settlement; execution continues or the invocation exits nonzero/cancelled.

The contract should make this narrow meaning unmistakable. v0.1 may use a hard cut; do not preserve a prose-only compatibility leaf. Partial commits without an accepted `completed` or `partially_completed` Receipt are merely crash/cancellation residue for the caller to inspect, not a successful or refused settlement.

Fixer currently has no output compliance audit; structural validation directly terminates. Add a fresh same-active-model Fixer compliance audit after structural validation and before acceptance. It checks only that completed/refused per-finding dispositions and the overall status demonstrably follow the Fixer Soul, sealed packet, and invocation record; it is not a second Fixer and does not re-adjudicate findings. `revise` returns control for corrected resubmission. Audit authentication/provider/malformed-decision/transport failure aborts the invocation nonzero and cannot manufacture refusal. Recorder must preserve accepted audit observation consistently with existing audited roles.